<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pancho Villa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Francisco Villa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pancho Villa was a Mexican revolutionary general from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> who led the Division of the North in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a>. He helped overthrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoriano-huerta">Victoriano Huerta</a> in 1914, but after breaking with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venustiano-carranza">Venustiano Carranza</a> he was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvaro-obregon">Álvaro Obregón</a> at Celaya in 1915. His raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, in 1916 provoked the American Punitive Expedition, and he was assassinated in 1923.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pancho-villa.jpg" alt="Pancho Villa" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: D.W. Hoffman, an El Paso photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Francisco_Villa_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Villa told conflicting stories about his early life. According to most sources he was born on 5 June 1878 as José Doroteo Arango Arámbula, the eldest of five children of a sharecropper, Agustín Arango, and Micaela Arámbula. He grew up on the Rancho de la Coyotada, one of the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">haciendas</a> in Durango, and had little schooling beyond basic literacy. He worked as a sharecropper, muleskinner, butcher, bricklayer and foreman for a US railway company. His dictated memoirs say that he became a hill-country thief after killing a hacienda owner who had raped his sister; the record shows that on 22 September 1894 he shot the man in the foot.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Villa joined the rebellion that Francisco Madero called in 1910 against President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a>, after Abraham González reached out to him in Chihuahua, and Madero promoted him to colonel. After Madero was murdered in the coup of February 1913, Villa joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venustiano-carranza">Venustiano Carranza</a>'s Constitutionalist Army against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoriano-huerta">Victoriano Huerta</a>. That year local commanders elected him provisional governor of Chihuahua against Carranza's wishes, and he carried out a significant land reform, confiscating estates and expelling their owners. In 1914 Carranza diverted him from a direct advance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, and Carranza's army entered the capital first in August.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huerta">Huerta</a>'s fall, Villa and Emiliano Zapata allied against Carranza after the Convention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aguascalientes">Aguascalientes</a>, and a civil war followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvaro-obregon">Álvaro Obregón</a> decisively defeated Villa in 1915, and he split his remaining forces into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> bands. At Namiquipa he rounded up the wives of men who had formed a home guard and allowed his soldiers to rape them, although some historians argue that crimes he did not commit were attributed to him.</p><p>On 9 March 1916 nearly 100 of his men attacked Columbus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, in which 18 Americans and about 80 Villistas died. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">President Wilson</a> sent 5,000 soldiers under General Frederick Funston, and John Pershing's force, using aircraft and trucks, pursued Villa fruitlessly until February 1917.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Carranza's fall in 1920 Villa negotiated an amnesty with the interim president Adolfo de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huerta">Huerta</a> and received a landed estate on condition that he retire from politics. On 20 July 1923 he was shot dead in an ambush in Parral, most likely on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarco-elias-calles">Plutarco Elías Calles</a> and President Obregón. His body was stolen from its grave in 1926 and the head removed. In 1976 his remains were reburied in the Monument to the Revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>.</p><p>He is generally regarded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> as a hero who stood up to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the subject of corridos, films and novels, while some American media call him a murderer. His biographer Friedrich Katz judged that his time as governor of Chihuahua might in some ways be called the first welfare state in Mexico.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pancho Villa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Madero’s murder in 1913, the Constitutionalists of Venustiano Carranza, Álvaro Obregón and <b>Pancho Villa</b>, together with the Zapatistas of Morelos, defeated Victoriano Huerta, and Obregón then beat Villa at the battles of Celaya in 1915.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The best-known verses today are two of <b>Pancho Villa</b>'s soldiers, aimed at President Victoriano Huerta: in them the cockroach stands for Huerta, who was known as a heavy drinker and was held a traitor for his part in the death of the revolutionary president Francisco Madero.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-cucaracha">La Cucaracha</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in San Juan del Río del Centauro del Norte, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in Hidalgo del Parral, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.78,-104.46&amp;year=1907" aria-label="San Juan del Río del Centauro del Norte on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pancho Villa"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3889 5376h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3889 5376h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.78,-104.46&amp;year=1907">What was here in 1907? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
